- (pg36 1.2.1 Step 1a: Determine the risk group for each of the activities carried out in the building refer to Table 1.1 and Paragraph 1.1.1)
- (pg36 1.2.1 Step 1a: If the activity is not listed explicitly, choose the nearest suitable risk group)
- (pg36 1.2.1 Step 1b: If there is more than one risk group for a firecell, determine its primary risk group)
- (pg36 1.2.1 Step 1b: Primary risk group is the one with the most onerous fire safety requirements)
- (pg36 1.2.1 Step 2a: Establish the relevant building measurements including building height, floor area, wall openings and distances to relevant boundaries)
- (pg36 1.2.1 Step 2b: Work out the occupant loads for the relevant occupied spaces refer to Paragraph 1.4)
- (pg36 1.2.1 Step 3: Satisfy the fire safety requirements of this Acceptable Solution refer to Parts 2-7 based on the occupant loads and on the building's dimensions and features where required)
- (pg36 1.2.2: If a building contains activities which individually may be categorised in different risk groups, the risk group designated for a particular firecell shall be that of the primary risk group)
- (pg36 1.2.2: The primary risk group shall be that one within the firecell that has the most onerous fire safety requirements)
- (pg36 1.2.2: Other risk groups may be incorporated within the same space provided these are ancillary to and support the primary risk groups)
- (pg36 1.2.3: Depending on the particular building and the uses or activities, there may be several primary risk groups with one or more on each floor)
- (pg36 1.3.1: This Acceptable Solution may be used to determine the compliance of building work in relation to an existing building)
- (pg37 1.4.1: The occupant load shall be determined from the risk group and number of people in each space of the building)
- (pg37 1.4.1: The occupant load may need to be evaluated for a space or open floor area involving one or more activities)
- (pg37 1.4.1: The occupant load may need to be evaluated for a floor containing more than one risk group)
- (pg37 1.4.1: The occupant load may need to be evaluated for a single firecell)
- (pg37 1.4.1: The occupant load may need to be evaluated for each floor within a firecell)
- (pg37 1.4.2: Occupant loads shall be calculated from the occupant densities given in Table 1.2 based on the floor area)
- (pg37 1.4.2: The occupant densities in Table 1.2 already allow for a proportion of the floor area appropriate to the activity being occupied by furniture, partitions, fixtures and associated equipment)
- (pg37 1.4.2: If a building space has alternative activity uses, the activity having the greatest occupant density shall be used)
- (pg37 1.4.2: If an activity is not specifically described in Table 1.2, the nearest reasonable description shall be used)
- (pg37 1.4.3: Duplication shall be avoided by ensuring that where people may be involved in more than one activity, they are counted only once)
- (pg37 1.4.3: Do not include an occupant load for exitways)
- (pg37 1.4.3: Do not include the occupant load determined for areas such as lift lobbies or sanitary facilities that are used intermittently by people already counted elsewhere in the building)
- (pg37 1.4.4: Occupant load assessment shall take account of the actual arrangement and number of seats for fixed seating see Paragraph 3.7.4)
- (pg37 1.4.4: Where additional floor area abuts the fixed seating, additional occupants are permitted in that floor area based on standing space density provided the escape route is not obstructed)
- (pg37 1.4.5: The requirements account for the fact that other people may be present in the building or firecell and additional calculations are not required when an occupant load is derived by bed spaces for risk groups SM and SI)
- (pg37 1.4.6: For risk group SI the term 'bed' means the number of people that are under care or detention)
- (pg37 1.4.6: For risk group SI bed can include people on beds, recliner or lounge chairs, dentist chairs, treatment tables, or any other furniture where an occupant may be for a period of treatment, in care or detention)
- (pg37 1.4.7: If the occupant load derived from Table 1.2 is clearly more than that which will occur, the basis of any proposal for a lesser occupant load shall be substantiated to the building consent authority)
- (pg37 1.4.8: If the maximum occupant load is greater than that calculated from Table 1.2, the higher number shall be used as the basis for the fire safety design)
- (pg37 1.4.8: Occupant load greater than Table 1.2 will need to be justified to the building consent authority)
